text = "1. Introduction\n\nThis includes a rich heritage of medicinal plants, with over 1,200 species documented (Adewale Ahmed, 2022) and many more awaiting formal study and conservation (Jantan, 2004).\n\nDespite this abundance, many species remain under-researched and under-promoted as sustainable food sources (Ahmad et al., 2015).\n\nParallel to the global trend, Malaysia is experiencing a noticeable decline in traditional knowledge about local herbs, especially among its youth (Sayok & Teucher, 2018).\n\nA growing preference for fast food and convenience meals is eroding traditional culinary practices and diminishing this cultural heritage (Kamardan et al., 2024).\n\nThis dietary shift not only affects personal health but also severs the critical connection between communities and their natural environment.\n\nReversing this trend requires innovative approaches to reintegrate these valuable plants into modern diets and educational initiatives, thereby advancing both ecological sustainability and national food security.\n\nGiven the growing need to preserve botanical heritage and traditional plant knowledge among younger generations, innovative technological solutions are emerging as powerful tools in environmental education.\n\nThe integration of advanced technologies, particularly in mobile applications for plant recognition, has gained significant traction in recent years (Weng et al., 2017; Roopashree & Anitha, 2021; Sun et al., 2022; Fernandes et al., 2023).\n\nHowever, many existing applications focus primarily on identification, often overlooking the equally important aspects of nutritional value and culinary application.\n\nThis gap highlights a valuable opportunity to develop more holistic platforms that not only recognize herbs and plants but also provide users with practical guidance on their nutritional benefits and suggested uses in everyday cooking.\n\nBy bridging this gap, such tools can enhance public awareness of healthy dietary practices, foster sustainable food choices, and reconnect users with traditional culinary and ecological knowledge.\n\nThese applications can be especially impactful in an era dominated by processed and fast foods, offering a pathway toward healthier lifestyles and stronger connections to nature and cultural heritage.\n\nThis study introduces an AI-powered mobile application, PlantPals, tailored for youths, enabling them to recognize local edible plants through simple photo-capture functionality.\n\nThe app will offer comprehensive information on the nutritional value and health benefits of these herbs, alongside practical cooking guidance to facilitate their integration into everyday meals.\n\nBeyond its educational content, the application aims to influence users’ dietary habits and attitudes toward sustainable food practices.\n\nThe paper begins with a literature review of existing mobile plant recognition applications, as well as relevant learning theories in environmental education."
